---
import LegalServiceLayout from '../../components/LegalServiceLayout.astro';
const minorTypes = [
  { title: 'Full', description: 'A broader transfer of care and decision-making authority when the statutory requirements are met.' },
  { title: 'Limited', description: 'Authority tailored to the child’s needs and the powers identified in the court order.' },
  { title: 'Temporary', description: 'Time-limited authority to address a qualifying temporary need.' },
  { title: 'Emergency', description: 'An expedited, short-term response when the statutory emergency standard is satisfied.' },
];
---
<LegalServiceLayout title="Wisconsin Adult & Minor Guardianship Attorney" description="Virtual Wisconsin legal guidance for minor guardianships under Chapters 48 and 54 and adult guardianships of the person and estate under Chapter 54." canonicalPath="/legal-services/guardianships" breadcrumbLabel="Guardianships" headline="Protecting the Vulnerable: Wisconsin Guardianship Services" subheadline="Compassionate, professional virtual legal guidance to secure decision-making authority for the children and adults you love." ctaHeading="Discuss the protection your loved one needs.">
  <section class="container-shell py-16 sm:py-24" aria-labelledby="minor-title"><h2 id="minor-title" class="max-w-4xl text-3xl leading-tight text-forest sm:text-4xl">1. Minor Guardianships (Wis. Stat. Ch. 48 &amp; 54)</h2><p class="mt-7 max-w-4xl text-lg leading-9 text-onyx/80">A minor guardianship can provide trusted decision-making when a child needs legal protection. Full, limited, temporary, and emergency guardianships of the person generally proceed under Wis. Stat. § 48.9795, with different requirements for each type.</p><div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-4">{minorTypes.map((type) => <article class="border border-forest/20 bg-white p-7"><h3 class="text-2xl text-forest">{type.title}</h3><p class="mt-5 text-base leading-8 text-onyx/80">{type.description}</p></article>)}</div><div class="mt-8 border-l-4 border-gold bg-forest/5 p-7"><h3 class="text-2xl text-forest">Guardianship of a Minor’s Estate</h3><p class="mt-5 max-w-4xl text-base leading-8 text-onyx/80">A minor’s property or financial affairs may require a guardian of the estate under Chapter 54. The person and estate proceedings are distinct statutory paths; the same four Chapter 48 categories do not automatically apply to estate guardianships.</p></div></section>
  <section class="border-y border-gold/30 bg-forest-dark py-16 text-parchment sm:py-24" aria-labelledby="adult-title"><div class="container-shell"><h2 id="adult-title" class="max-w-4xl text-3xl leading-tight sm:text-4xl">2. Adult Guardianships (Wis. Stat. Ch. 54)</h2><p class="mt-7 max-w-4xl text-lg leading-9 text-parchment/85">Developmental disabilities, severe illness, or dementia may create a need for help with decisions. A diagnosis alone does not establish the grounds for guardianship. Chapter 54 requires specific findings about decision-making ability and whether less restrictive alternatives can meet the individual’s needs.</p><div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2"><article class="border border-gold/30 bg-forest p-7 sm:p-9"><h3 class="text-3xl text-gold-light">Guardianship of the Person</h3><p class="mt-5 text-base leading-8 text-parchment/80">Addresses authorized decisions affecting personal care, health, and safety. The court determines the guardian’s powers and the rights the individual retains.</p></article><article class="border border-gold/30 bg-forest p-7 sm:p-9"><h3 class="text-3xl text-gold-light">Guardianship of the Estate</h3><p class="mt-5 text-base leading-8 text-parchment/80">Addresses authorized management of property and financial affairs. The scope of authority is tailored to the person’s needs and the court’s findings.</p></article></div></div></section>
</LegalServiceLayout>
